== fuller page ==
Ask the c4 tree one question, "what are C4's run-levels?", and more than
one witness answers. Pin keeps them side by side.

Spoke's ladder is Corwin's own ghostwheel code. It counts what is standing
up on a host: 0 bootstrapping, 1 containers, 2 components, 3
configurations, 4 connections, 5 running [code]. Pin's ladder is the
language document, C4-LANG. It counts a lifecycle: bootstrapping,
containers, then configure, make, install, setup, self-test, enablement
[lang].

Two more answer too. A fact from 2026-09-11 counts locks, 0 read, 1
present, 2 fresh, 3 digest, 4 signed, and calls everything above them
virtual [tumbler]. And the ladder on spread seven counts where a broken
promise is caught [promise].

The first two agree at the bottom: bootstrapping, then containers. Then
they part. One counts what is up; the other counts what has been done to
the software. Neither is wrong. That they differ tells us where each one
stands: a host, a build, a lock, a promise. (That reading is Pin's, not
Corwin's.)

His paper names the method: where two readings diverge, "that gap is not a
defect in the argument. It is the argument's instrument" [instrument]. The
shop's rule for measures that disagree is the same: show them side by side,
"which is not a score" [crown].

Part Two climbs Spoke's ladder, one rung at a time, as a ghostwheel wakes
up.

